// The sqlite3 binding for sysl -- a package, in the sense `design/packages.md` gives the word.
//
// This is the first sysl package that lives outside the compiler's own repository, so it is also the
// first real reader of this file. There is no `dependencies` block because the binding depends on no
// other sysl package; what it needs is a C library, and `@link("sqlite3")` in the module header is
// where that is said.
//
// This is the block a *consumer* writes to reach this package, and resolution for it is built
// (`design/packages.md` §§ 3-6): a `sysl build` fetches the coordinate, selects by MVS, checks what
// arrived against `sysl.sum`, and compiles it as more modules.
//
//     dependencies {
//       sqlite3 { git = "github.com/sysl-lang/sqlite3", version = "0.6.0" }
//     }
//
// The coordinate is identity rather than a URL, so it carries no `https://`, and the version is the
// tag `v0.6.0` on this repository. Note that the coordinate names the *package*, `sqlite3`, while the
// module a program imports is `sh.sysl.sqlite` -- `packages.md § 9` keeps those two deliberately
// apart. `build-lib` and `--lib` still work, but they are no longer the only way in.

package {
  name    = "sqlite3"
  version = "0.6.0"
}

// SQLite is a hosted library: it opens files, and on this path it reaches the C runtime for them. A
// build for a freestanding target has no operating system to do that, and saying so here is what
// turns "this will not work" into one clean refusal before anything is compiled.
//
// `heap` is deliberately NOT required. The binding makes strings, so it does allocate -- but the
// requirement a package states is about the *environment*, and a program that cannot allocate will
// be told so at the construction that needs it, in the module that wrote it, which is a better place
// than here.
//
// **`headers` is the other kind of thing a package can need of its environment.** A capability is
// answered by the target and there is nothing for anybody to go and do; a header is answered by a
// path on a machine this package has never seen. `c/c.sysl` says `@include("<sqlite3.h>")` and no
// copy of that header is in this repository, so a build has to be told where one is.
//
// **It is declared even though macOS already has it**, and that is the whole reason this is worth a
// paragraph. `sqlite3.h` is in the macOS SDK, on a path clang searches without being asked, so on
// this machine the flag points at a directory that was already being read. It is not a spare check:
// a Linux box without `libsqlite3-dev` is the case, and there the refusal arrives before clang and
// names what to install instead of naming a file that is missing.
//
// The check asks what a build *says* it has rather than what it might happen to find, so a bare
// `--include-path` deliberately does not answer this one -- `packages.md § headers` says why, and it
// is what stops a declaration from being satisfied by accident on one machine and unenforced on the
// next. The cost is the flag, on every platform, and it is accepted.
//
// A path is not written here. This file is committed and describes the *package*, and where a
// machine keeps its headers is not a property of the package -- `15 §8` refuses it in as many words,
// which is the same split `@link("sqlite3")` above draws against `--link-path`.
requires {
  os = true
  headers {
    sqlite3 = "SQLite's own header, which this binding does not carry -- the macOS SDK has it, and a Linux box wants libsqlite3-dev"
  }
}
